 "drivingQuestion": "What is the reason of Beit Shammai that two males suffice?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Beit Shammai's reason given and defended; the answer is completed in the next unit.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.61b.18",
   "daf": "61b",
   "sentenceId": 17,
   "he": "בֵּית שַׁמַּאי אוֹמְרִים: שְׁנֵי זְכָרִים. מַאי טַעְמַיְיהוּ דְּבֵית שַׁמַּאי? יָלְפִינַן מִמֹּשֶׁה, דִּכְתִיב: ״בְּנֵי מֹשֶׁה גֵּרְשׁוֹם וֶאֱלִיעֶזֶר״. וּבֵית הִלֵּל: יָלְפִינַן מִבְּרִיָּיתוֹ שֶׁל עוֹלָם. וּבֵית שַׁמַּאי, לֵילְפֵי מִבְּרִיָּיתוֹ שֶׁל עוֹלָם? אֵין דָּנִין אֶפְשָׁר",
   "en": "§ The mishna states that «Beit Shammai say» that one fulfills the mitzva to be fruitful and multiply when he has «two males.» The Gemara asks: «What is the reason of Beit Shammai?» The Gemara answers: «We learn» this «from Moses as it is written: “The sons of Moses, Gershom and Eliezer”» (I Chronicles 23:15). Since Moses did not have any other children, two sons must be sufficient to fulfill the mitzva. «And» the reason of «Beit Hillel» is that «we learn from the creation of the world,» as mankind was created male and female. The Gemara asks: «And Beit Shammai, let them learn from the creation of the world» as well. The Gemara answers that Beit Shammai could say to you: «We do not derive» a case where it is «possible»"
  }
 ],
